\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Tests for a resolved symbol}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{k} be an ordered field and #{f:R\to k^n} a map on the \vocabk{simplicial momentum grid}{fdip-013X}. Its interpolant is \vocabk{resolved}{fdip-015I} if and only if no edge joins two zero vertices and, for every simplex without
a zero vertex, the origin is not a convex combination of the values of #{f} at its vertices.
These are finite tests over #{k}, with faces of every dimension included. If the vertex
zeros are nondegenerate, the sum of their degrees is then zero.}

\p{Suppose that #{f_j(r)=g_j(r_j)} depends only on the coordinate #{r_j}, and that each
#{g_j} has a zero sample. Then #{\tilde f} is resolved if and only if no #{g_j} has two
cyclically adjacent samples of strictly opposite sign or two cyclically adjacent zero samples.}

\proof{
\p{A zero edge is zero throughout. On a simplex without zero vertices,
a convex combination equal to zero gives a zero different from all its
vertices. Conversely, take the positive barycentric support of a zero.
If any support values are nonzero, remove all zero values and divide
the other weights by their positive sum. This represents zero on a face
without zero vertices, contrary to the test. Thus every support vertex
is zero. Any two of them would span a zero edge, so the support is a
single vertex. With nondegenerate vertex zeros, \ref{fdip-0140} now gives
the degree sum since there is no off-grid contribution.}

\p{In a lifted grid simplex each coordinate takes at most two adjacent
sample values. If these never have strictly opposite signs, a weighted
coordinate sum can vanish only if all its positive-weight terms vanish.
Applying this in every coordinate makes all support vertices zero.
Two distinct such vertices would differ in a coordinate with adjacent
zero samples. Conversely, fix zero samples in the other coordinates.
Two opposite strict signs give an interior zero of the intervening axis
edge by linear interpolation. Two adjacent zero samples give a whole
zero edge. This reasoning includes the edge across a periodic seam.}
}
